    doc.append('## 1. Introduction</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('Vascular access is an essential component of inpatient and outpatient management across critical care, emergency medicine, cardiology, anesthesiology, nephrology, radiology, and oncology. Indications span intravenous fluid administration, hemodynamic monitoring, continuous vasopressor infusion, parenteral nutrition, hemodialysis, and complex percutaneous cardiovascular interventions (e.g., transcatheter valve replacement, mechanical circulatory support).')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('Historically, vascular access relied entirely on surface anatomical landmarks and arterial palpation. However, blind puncture is constrained by extensive inter-individual anatomical variations, pathological vessel displacement, thrombosis, vessel spasm, hypovolemia, and obesity. Blind attempts lead to mechanical complications in up to 15% of patients, including unintended arterial puncture, hematoma formation, pneumothorax, hemothorax, pseudoaneurysm, and nerve injury.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('The introduction of point-of-care ultrasound has revolutionized vascular access. Real-time ultrasonography visualizes target vessels, defines adjacent anatomical hazard structures, confirms intraluminal needle entry, tracks guidewire trajectory, and verifies proper catheter seating. This 2025 document provides updated, evidence-based recommendations for ultrasound-guided vascular cannulation in both adult and pediatric populations, defining standardized techniques and minimum training requirements.')
    doc.append('')
